   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Loneliness (UCLS) causes Perceived stress (PSS) through a direct causal mechanism. Increased loneliness can heighten an individual's perception of stress due to reduced social support and greater emotional burden. Conversely, lower loneliness leads to lower perceived stress. This direct, positive relationship is supported by established psychological theories and is not fully mediated by other variables in the provided CLD.", 'session_id': 'aaf0c142-c72b-4e38-8b25-2754d426018b', 'prob_std': 0.3175187426883103, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7161293055312706, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes Perceived stress (PSS) because individuals who experience higher levels of loneliness tend to have more unstructured time, which directly increases their exposure to stressful life events. As a result, the absence of frequent social interactions inherently leads to a greater number of daily stressors, thereby raising Perceived stress (PSS). Conversely, those with lower Loneliness (UCLS) have structured routines that shield them from such stressors, resulting in lower Perceived stress (PSS). This direct positive relationship is consistent with behavioral models suggesting that social engagement prevents the occurrence of stress-inducing situations.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes Perceived stress (PSS) because individuals who experience higher levels of loneliness tend to have more unstructured time, which directly increases their exposure to stressful life events. As a result, the absence of frequent social interactions inherently leads to a greater number of daily stressors, thereby raising Perceived stress (PSS). Conversel