134. Physical inactivity (SBQ)-[POSITIVE]->Loneliness (UCLS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) causes Loneliness (UCLS) in a direct causal relationship. Increased physical inactivity can reduce opportunities for social engagement and interaction, thereby directly increasing feelings of loneliness. Conversely, greater physical activity tends to facilitate social contact, which can reduce loneliness. This relationship holds even without considering mediating variables in the system.', 'session_id': 'aaf0c142-c72b-4e38-8b25-2754d426018b', 'prob_std': 0.31203325820759437, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2791498/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/risk-factors/index.html', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666518223000451', 'https://academic.oup.com/aje/article/193/7/996/7601692'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7282703157468026, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A plausible mechanism underlying the positive relationship between Physical inactivity (SBQ) and Loneliness (UCLS) is that individuals who are less physically active may experience physiological changes that directly trigger feelings of loneliness. Reduced physical movement can alter neurochemical balances, such as serotonin and dopamine levels, which are known to influence mood and social perception. As a result, higher scores on Physical inactivity (SBQ) are likely to produce an immediate increase in Loneliness (UCLS), independent of social context or external interactions, as the neurobiological effects of inactivity foster a subjective sense of isolation.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'A plausible mechanism underlying the positive relationship between Physical inactivity (SBQ) and Loneliness (UCLS) is that individuals who are less physically active may experience physiological changes that directly trigger feelings of loneliness. Reduced physical movement can alter neurochemical balances, such as serotonin and dopamine levels, which are known to influence