138. Physical inactivity (SBQ)-[POSITIVE]->Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Increased physical inactivity can lead to higher risk of depressive symptoms through mechanisms such as reduced endorphin release, lower social interaction, and disrupted circadian rhythms. Conversely, decreased physical inactivity (i.e., more physical activity) causally reduces depressive symptoms. This relationship is not fully mediated by other variables present in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'aaf0c142-c72b-4e38-8b25-2754d426018b', 'prob_std': 0.26115097188738284, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2022/22_0003.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7527477/', 'https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanpsy/article/PIIS2215-0366(20)30034-1/fulltext', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35416941/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7589582770604637, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) primarily because extended periods of sedentary behavior limit exposure to natural daylight, which in turn directly disrupts serotonin synthesis in the brain and precipitates depressive symptoms. Individuals who are more physically inactive typically spend less time outdoors, and this reduced daylight exposure alone is sufficient to explain the increase in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), independent of other lifestyle or psychosocial factors. Thus, interventions targeting physical inactivity (SBQ) can alleviate depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) chiefly by increasing natural light exposure, regardless of other changes in behavior or mood regulation strategies.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) primarily because extended periods of sedentary behavior limit exposure to natural daylight, which in turn directly disrupts serotonin synthesis in the brain and precipitates depressive symptoms. Individuals who are